Subject: Size a field through a typedef, so a replaced type still pads

`<div rustbindgen replaces="X"></div>` tells bindgen to bind `X` as some other
type the header declares, and that other type is usually a different size: the
annotation exists to substitute a Rust-shaped stand-in for a C++ type Rust
cannot express. bindgen already knows how to keep the layout of a struct with
such a field honest. It records the replacement as a `ResolvedTypeRef` on the
replaced type, and `FieldData::codegen` resolves the field's type through
`ResolvedTypeRef`s before asking `StructLayoutTracker` for the field's layout,
so the tracker sees the size the emitted Rust field really has and pads the
struct out to the size clang reported for it.

A typedef in between defeats that. `ItemResolver` deliberately does not follow
`Alias`, so

    struct Real { unsigned long a; unsigned long b; unsigned long c; };
    /** <div rustbindgen replaces="Real"></div> */
    struct Stand { void* p; };
    typedef Real my_real;
    struct A { my_real a; };
    struct B { Real a; };

pads `B` by sixteen bytes and `A` not at all, though C++ calls their one field
the same type. The tracker was handed the typedef, whose layout is the one
clang computed - the size of `Real`, not the size of `Stand`, which is what the
field is written as - concluded the field filled the struct, and emitted no
trailing padding. `A` is then a third of the width of the object C++ hands it,
and every object crossing the boundary is truncated.

Follow aliases as well, for the layout query alone. Everything else the arm does
with the field's type - the incomplete-array and flexible-array-member checks,
the type name it reports through `ParseCallbacks` - keeps looking at the type
the field was declared with, which is the type those want.

The walk stops where codegen writes something sized from the alias rather than
from what it aliases. Two of those are exactly the conditions the `Alias` arms
of `TryToRustTy for Type` and `CodeGenerator for Type` test: an alias which is
opaque, written as a blob of its own layout, and an alias named for a type Rust
already has, `uint64_t` and `size_t` among them, written as that type whatever
it was declared over. The third is deliberately wider than the arm it guards -
an aliased type with no layout is written as a blob of the alias's layout only
once conversion has failed - but a type with no layout has no answer to give a
layout question either, so the walk stops on the missing layout alone. It also
stops on a repeated item, so a typedef cycle cannot spin.

Addresses the bug reported as rust-lang/rust-bindgen#3158.

